1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,840
They're passageways to the underworld,

2
00:00:03,940 --> 00:00:07,140
where evil lurks and the devil plays.

3
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:10,040
Yet today we travel in them, work in them,

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,140
build cities over them.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:15,040
We can't live without them, so we defy nature,

6
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:19,660
lose fortunes, and risk lives to build them deeper and longer.

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:23,460
Now sink into the world of tunnels on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,500
Let's go.

9
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:50,080
Nobody knows exactly how many miles of tunnel

10
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:51,660
there are in the world.

11
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:54,560
Their combined distance has never been measured.

12
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:57,760
But it's speculated that if they were all laid end to end,

13
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:01,840
stretch from the Earth to the moon.

14
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:04,400
Today, tunnels are often taken for granted,

15
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,840
even though they are indispensable to the modern world.

16
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,240
They were vital to the ancients as well.

17
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:12,200
And their development can be traced

18
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,140
from the earliest civilizations.

19
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:30,800
The ancient city of Babylon was divided in half

20
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:33,500
by the Euphrates River.

21
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:36,400
Each portion of the city was protected from invaders

22
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,200
by impenetrable walls.

23
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,440
The royal palace was located on one side.

24
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:44,340
Important religious shrines and temples

25
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:46,740
were located on the other.

26
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:49,340
In order for the royal family to visit these sites,

27
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:51,920
they were forced to leave the safety of the city's

28
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:54,480
fortifications and expose themselves

29
00:01:54,481 --> 00:01:59,880
to the ever-present danger of attack by enemy nations.

30
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:03,480
Historians tell us that sometime between the years 2180

31
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:06,920
and 2160 BC, the rulers of Babylon

32
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:11,240
decided to solve this problem once and for all.

33
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:13,240
They ordered a tunnel to be constructed

34
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:17,240
between the two halves of the city.

35
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,680
The Babylonians were by no means strangers

36
00:02:19,780 --> 00:02:21,980
to projects of this magnitude.

37
00:02:21,981 --> 00:02:25,320
The walled city itself was a testament to their engineering

38
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:26,120
skill.

39
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,320
In order to build the tunnel, they first

40
00:02:31,420 --> 00:02:33,720
diverted the course of the Euphrates River

41
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,020
by constructing a dam, forcing it to flow around the city

42
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,340
rather than through it.

43
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:43,040
Next, they excavated a trench across the now dry riverbed

44
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:47,280
and extended it under the walls on either side.

45
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:49,740
They lined and roofed the trench with bricks, which

46
00:02:49,741 --> 00:02:52,980
which were then waterproofed with tar.

47
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:54,740
When they removed the temporary dam

48
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:58,080
and allowed the river to flow back along its natural course,

49
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:01,880
the whole tunnel structure was covered by water.

50
00:03:01,980 --> 00:03:05,380
Babylonians could now travel safely from one side of the river

51
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:07,400
to the other.

52
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:11,560
The entire endeavor reputedly took 20 years to complete.

53
00:03:11,660 --> 00:03:14,800
Even though there is no physical trace of that tunnel left

54
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:17,440
in what is now Iraq, this is the earliest

55
00:03:17,441 --> 00:03:20,080
constructed for the purpose of transportation.

56
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,280
The construction approach used by the Babylonians

57
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:30,020
is still in use today.

58
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:32,560
It is known as cut and cover.

59
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,660
It is frequently the most practical, most economical,

60
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:39,020
and safest way to build a tunnel if the proposed route

61
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:40,360
is close to the surface.

62
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:47,000
On the other hand, you can't build a cut and cover tunnel

63
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:50,500
through a mountain without removing the mountain itself.

64
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,400
And since many of the world's tunnels

65
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:56,100
have been built in order to tame these natural barriers,

66
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:59,140
early tunnel builders wrestled with the problem of how

67
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:03,240
to best slice through the compacted, unforgiving rock

68
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:04,040
of mountains.

69
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:09,460
One technique used by the Egyptians

70
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:13,260
and later by the Romans was called spalling or fire

71
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:14,760
quenching.

72
00:04:14,761 --> 00:04:17,560
It called for heating the rock face with fire,

73
00:04:17,660 --> 00:04:20,400
and then rapidly cooling it with water,

74
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:21,860
causing the rock to crack.

75
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:29,300
This technique was extremely hazardous for the workers.

76
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:32,520
The fire consumed the oxygen in the tunnel,

77
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:42,360
and the quenching produced scalding fumes and steam.

78
00:04:42,361 --> 00:04:47,800
So it was not much fun to be involved in that process.

79
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:51,540
And I'm sure a lot of people perished in the process

80
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:54,380
of building these tunnels.

81
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,600
The Romans were industrious builders of tunnels, many of

82
00:04:57,700 --> 00:05:00,040
which are still in use today.

83
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:03,680
They built tunnels to supplement their elevated aqueducts,

84
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:06,280
used to carry water over great distances

85
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:08,920
to major population centers.

86
00:05:08,921 --> 00:05:12,120
They also constructed sophisticated sewer tunnels,

87
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:15,260
such as this one in what is today Morocco.

88
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:18,560
Tunnels were also built to divert water for irrigation

89
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:20,860
and flood control.

90
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:23,500
But perhaps the best known Roman tunnels

91
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,740
were built for the transportation of men.

92
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:29,300
The Roman road tunnels, which were built in Campania

93
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,280
in southern Italy, are really the progenitor

94
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:35,780
of all other transportation tunnels.

95
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,920
But they're rather late technological development.

96
00:05:38,921 --> 00:05:46,320
They were built in 36 BC and during the Roman Civil War

97
00:05:46,420 --> 00:05:55,300
by Octavian, who conceived them originally for military use.

98
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:57,680
The decisions about how to build a tunnel

99
00:05:57,780 --> 00:06:00,300
are always governed by the site.

100
00:06:00,400 --> 00:06:03,220
The type and consistency of the earth through which the tunnel

101
00:06:03,320 --> 00:06:07,480
must pass is of paramount importance.

102
00:06:07,481 --> 00:06:09,720
When tunneling through hard rock, for instance,

103
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:11,380
the walls and ceiling of the tunnel

104
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:13,520
have a great deal of stand-up time.

105
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:16,560
In other words, the rock is strong enough to stand up,

106
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:21,620
even when material is cleared away to create the passage.

107
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:24,700
Hard rock may have a stand-up time of many years or even

108
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:28,660
centuries without man-made supports.

109
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:31,200
Layered rock may require moderate support

110
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,440
to relieve stress on fragile seams in the formation,

111
00:06:34,441 --> 00:06:38,580
especially where water has seeped into it.

112
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:40,740
And for gravel, soft earth, and sand,

113
00:06:40,840 --> 00:06:43,680
which may have almost no stand-up time at all,

114
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:48,280
supports must be erected immediately.

115
00:06:48,380 --> 00:06:51,020
You have to concern yourself with water.

116
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:56,300
That's probably the single most concern

117
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:03,800
on any tunneling engineer's mind, how to control the water.

118
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:06,140
Everything we know about tunnel building

119
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:08,240
was learned by trial and error.

120
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,640
But occasionally, men with inquisitive minds

121
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:12,540
and great powers of observation were

122
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:16,940
able to interpret lessons that nature has placed in plain sight.

123
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:23,520
In the case of tunnel building, the master teacher

124
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:27,160
was to be one of the humblest creatures found in the sea.

125
00:07:27,161 --> 00:07:32,160
After the fall of the Roman Empire,

126
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:37,160
the art of tunnel building was lost for centuries.

127
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:41,800
During the Dark Ages, cities on the whole declined in Europe.

128
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:47,120
And so the principal reasons for civic tunnels,

129
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:51,220
water supply and sewage, no longer existed.

130
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:54,220
And for the most part, whatever tunneling was done

131
00:07:54,221 --> 00:07:57,320
And for the most part, whatever tunneling was done

132
00:07:57,420 --> 00:08:04,200
was arranged by lords as escape routes from underneath

133
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:09,360
their castles or as sub-training hiding places.

134
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:14,300
So you have a kind of retrograde motion in the story

135
00:08:14,400 --> 00:08:18,040
of tunnel engineering during that period.

136
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:20,240
But with the birth of the Industrial Age,

137
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:23,180
the rise of cities and the resulting urban congestion

138
00:08:23,181 --> 00:08:26,620
created new demand for tunnels.

139
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:28,780
By the 1800s, the city of London

140
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:32,280
had become so dense with horse-drawn wagons, carriages,

141
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:34,920
and omnibuses that its streets and bridges

142
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:38,520
were nearly impassable.

143
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:41,540
The population clamored for a new thoroughfare

144
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,240
across the River Thames.

145
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:47,380
The building of a bridge would have obstructed the river

146
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:50,480
and interrupted commerce.

147
00:08:50,481 --> 00:08:52,920
And so a tunnel was proposed.

148
00:08:53,020 --> 00:08:56,780
However, the cut and cover method employed by the Babylonians

149
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:58,880
would have been wildly impractical,

150
00:08:58,980 --> 00:09:00,660
since diverting the river would have meant

151
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:03,720
submerging part of the city.

152
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:05,920
Therefore, in order to build such a tunnel,

153
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:08,940
the builder would need to bore under the river.

154
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:11,760
No one had ever successfully driven a tunnel

155
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:14,440
through the soft silt and clay of a riverbed.

156
00:09:14,441 --> 00:09:22,440
And yet in 1802, excavation began on a tunnel beneath the River

157
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:23,240
Thames.

158
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:29,080
At first, the project seemed to progress at a remarkable pace,

159
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:32,100
despite the fact that the silt that formed the riverbed

160
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:34,000
was extremely soft and fluid.

161
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:38,300
Workmen erected timber supports in front of them

162
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:40,800
as the leading edge of the tunnel advanced.

163
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:43,240
There were freeways of the riverbed

164
00:09:43,241 --> 00:09:45,880
There were frequent cave-ins and minor floods,

165
00:09:45,980 --> 00:09:47,320
but these were brought under control

166
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:51,180
by dropping bags of clay from barges to plug the ruptures.

167
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:56,480
By January of 1808, the tunnel was

168
00:09:56,580 --> 00:09:58,980
within a few hundred feet of completion.

169
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:04,800
Then suddenly, disaster, another cave-in.

170
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:08,440
This time, the water rushed into the tunnel with such ferocity

171
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:10,940
that within 15 minutes, the river completely

172
00:10:10,941 --> 00:10:14,280
erased five years of work, as if the whole endeavor

173
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:17,240
had been no more substantial than a child's sandcastle.

174
00:10:20,140 --> 00:10:23,180
A subsequent inquiry determined that the building of a tunnel

175
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,380
under the Thames was impractical.

176
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,700
Among those who disagreed with this finding, however,

177
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,900
was a brilliant engineer and inventor

178
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,300
named Mark Isambard Brunel.

179
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,920
In the fall of 1816, while Brunel

180
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:43,040
was at work installing a sawmill,

181
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:47,080
he came upon a fragment of worm-eaten ship timber.

182
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:50,080
Upon close examination, he observed within it

183
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:54,180
the notorious shipworm, energetically destroying

184
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,780
its home.

185
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:57,960
This was not an unusual specimen,

186
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:00,260
for it was commonly acknowledged at the time

187
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:03,660
that shipworms were responsible for more damage

188
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:06,700
to the British fleet than all naval battles

189
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:08,200
England had ever fought.

190
00:11:10,940 --> 00:11:13,100
The shipworm, inappropriately named

191
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:15,780
because it's actually a type of clam,

192
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:19,580
has a hard protective shell on each side of its head.

193
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:23,620
The toothed edges of those shells can cut into wood,

194
00:11:23,720 --> 00:11:26,820
producing tiny powder-like shavings.

195
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:29,560
The worm consumes that powder, digests it,

196
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:31,800
and then excretes a substance that

197
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:38,100
forms a rigid shell-like lining in the tiny tunnel behind it.

198
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:43,440
From that moment onward, it became his study, as he said later,

199
00:11:43,441 --> 00:11:47,880
to find some mechanical means to imitate the action

200
00:11:47,881 --> 00:11:49,940
of this animal.

201
00:11:49,941 --> 00:11:53,340
After more than a year of observation and testing,

202
00:11:53,341 --> 00:11:58,480
in 1818, he patented the design for a large cast iron device

203
00:11:58,481 --> 00:12:01,460
he called a shield.

204
00:12:01,461 --> 00:12:05,600
Brunel's design resembled a huge metal can open at one end,

205
00:12:05,601 --> 00:12:08,900
inside of which the tunnel diggers worked, protected

206
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,500
from cave-ins.

207
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:14,400
36 individual cells were fitted into the front of the shield,

208
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:17,080
where the diggers, stacked in three tiers,

209
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:19,580
methodically excavated the soft earth.

210
00:12:22,420 --> 00:12:24,520
The front of the shield was fitted with holes

211
00:12:24,620 --> 00:12:26,360
that could be covered.

212
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:30,160
The workers uncovered the holes, dug out a few inches of earth,

213
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:32,620
recovered the holes, and then used powerful jacks

214
00:12:32,621 --> 00:12:36,360
to push the shield forward a few inches.

215
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:38,020
Meanwhile, another team of workers

216
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:41,020
built the reinforcing walls behind the shield,

217
00:12:41,120 --> 00:12:45,060
in the same way that a shipworm lines its tunnel.

218
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:48,860
And this was pretty revolutionary.

219
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:52,240
In fact, it was remarkably different than any kind

220
00:12:52,340 --> 00:12:56,840
of tunnel engineering which had ever been attempted.

221
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:59,540
An alliance of wealthy industrialists,

222
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:01,340
including the Duke of Wellington,

223
00:13:01,341 --> 00:13:03,980
were impressed by Brunel's design.

224
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:05,940
Inspired by the potential profits

225
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:08,140
to be reaped from the collection of tolls,

226
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:10,480
they agreed to finance the project.

227
00:13:10,580 --> 00:13:15,880
And so in 1825, Mark Brunel and his 23-year-old son,

228
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:19,300
Isambard, who served as resident engineer,

229
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:23,400
began construction of a new tunnel, a large double archway

230
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:29,040
suitable for vehicular traffic under the Thames River.

231
00:13:29,041 --> 00:13:32,140
It was a controversial venture from the outset.

232
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:34,980
From their pulpits, clergymen denounced the project

233
00:13:35,080 --> 00:13:38,080
as a flirtation with the underworld.

234
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:40,480
Others who had been involved with the earlier attempt

235
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:42,580
were keen to see the project fail.

236
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:49,400
Yet the general public was supportive, even enthusiastic,

237
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:53,100
and the construction site was a popular tourist attraction.

238
00:13:53,200 --> 00:13:55,600
Many foreign dignitaries visited the tunnel

239
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:57,900
and reported back to their governments.

240
00:13:57,901 --> 00:14:00,640
Brunel objected to these constant intrusions,

241
00:14:00,740 --> 00:14:03,840
but his investors were profiting by charging admission.

242
00:14:07,640 --> 00:14:10,440
Progress was slow and uneven.

243
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:12,820
The lack of adequate ventilation in the tunnel

244
00:14:12,920 --> 00:14:15,120
took its toll on the workers' health.

245
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:17,680
Noxious fumes from sulfur and hydrogen

246
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:20,260
gas that leached into the tunnel through the mud

247
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:24,200
caused inflammation of their eyes, skin diseases, nausea,

248
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:25,660
and dizziness.

249
00:14:25,661 --> 00:14:28,200
The number of hours a man could work in the tunnel

250
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:29,960
was severely limited.

251
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:32,000
Week after week, the tunnel's progress

252
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:34,860
could only be measured in inches.

253
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:38,400
Construction was frequently hampered by water seepage.

254
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:40,900
There were numerous calamities when the river

255
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,920
broke through the mud, flooding the tunnel.

256
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:49,420
One of these floods in January of 1827

257
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:52,460
was so severe that six workers drowned.

258
00:14:52,461 --> 00:14:55,200
It took 4,500 bags of clay and gravel

259
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:59,960
dropped from barges above to stop the flow of water.

260
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:03,400
By the summer of that year, the project ran out of funding.

261
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:06,040
The shield was bricked up in place,

262
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:09,480
and Brunel's brilliant career seemed to be over.

263
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:14,780
But the industrial growth of the 1830s

264
00:15:14,880 --> 00:15:16,660
and the proliferation of railroads

265
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:20,620
underscored the original reasons for building the tunnel.

266
00:15:20,621 --> 00:15:23,020
Before long, the profit potential of the project

267
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:27,260
was too strong to deny.

268
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,060
The King of England, William IV, became personally interested

269
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:32,960
in the work, and the Duke of Wellington

270
00:15:33,060 --> 00:15:37,980
obtained a loan of 46,000 pounds to complete the tunnel.

271
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:41,880
After a seven-year interruption, work on the tunnel began anew.

272
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:45,780
Brunel, now nearly 70 years old and in frail health,

273
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:49,780
continued to visit the tunnel daily to inspect the work

274
00:15:49,781 --> 00:15:53,580
and offer encouragement.

275
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:57,620
Finally, on March 25, 1843, nearly 18 years

276
00:15:57,720 --> 00:16:00,820
after the start of construction, the Thames Tunnel

277
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:02,220
opened to the public.

278
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:08,840
Within the first 24 hours, nearly 50,000 people

279
00:16:08,940 --> 00:16:11,940
sampled the novelty of being able to stroll leisurely

280
00:16:12,040 --> 00:16:13,940
beneath the river.

281
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:17,140
However, the jubilation was short-lived.

282
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:18,940
Since the operating company could not

283
00:16:18,941 --> 00:16:21,840
to build road connections, which would have made it accessible

284
00:16:21,940 --> 00:16:24,780
to carriage and car traffic, the tunnel proved

285
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:26,880
to be a white elephant.

286
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:29,280
The half penny toll collected from pedestrians

287
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:31,680
fell far short of the adequate compensation

288
00:16:31,780 --> 00:16:34,880
for the enormous investment.

289
00:16:34,980 --> 00:16:38,400
Eventually, the tunnel became a haven for London's homeless,

290
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:43,600
who took refuge in it from the rain and cold.

291
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:48,300
Finally, in 1865, it was purchased by the East London

292
00:16:48,301 --> 00:16:50,740
the East London Railroad, and tracks were laid.

293
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,240
Traveling through tunnels became a way of life

294
00:16:56,340 --> 00:16:57,240
for many Londoners.

295
00:17:00,040 --> 00:17:02,140
Parisians would soon follow suit

296
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,460
and began construction of an extensive underground subway

297
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:06,960
system.

298
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:09,460
And across the Atlantic, New York City

299
00:17:09,560 --> 00:17:13,800
was poised on the threshold of a new era that would give rise

300
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:17,400
to the most energetic tunnel building culture the world

301
00:17:17,401 --> 00:17:18,300
had ever seen.

302
00:17:26,140 --> 00:17:29,180
By the late 19th century, visionary engineers

303
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:32,420
such as England's James Henry Greathead,

304
00:17:32,520 --> 00:17:35,880
perhaps best known as the inventor of the roller skate,

305
00:17:35,980 --> 00:17:38,760
brought new ideas and refinements to the art

306
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:42,020
of tunnel construction.

307
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:44,960
Greathead's tunneling shield was smaller and sturdier

308
00:17:44,961 --> 00:17:47,500
than Brunel's, 12 feet in diameter,

309
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:54,900
and powered forward by hydraulic jacks with 210 tons of thrust.

310
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,200
Another development called for the injection of compressed air

311
00:17:58,300 --> 00:18:00,900
into a tunnel in order to counter the problem of water

312
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,020
leaks and cave-ins.

313
00:18:04,120 --> 00:18:07,620
Exerting 15 to 25 pounds of pressure per square inch

314
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:10,720
to the walls and ceiling, the pressurized environment

315
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:13,520
kept water and mud from pouring into the tunnel.

316
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:18,000
Airlocks allowed men and material to pass in and out

317
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:20,940
and still maintain the air pressure.

318
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:24,200
In and around New York, men who worked on such jobs

319
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:27,840
became known as sand hogs.

320
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:30,580
Well, it's a distinction that you work in tunnels.

321
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:32,680
And it's something that most people don't see.

322
00:18:32,780 --> 00:18:37,480
As a matter of fact, when my little girl was going to school,

323
00:18:37,580 --> 00:18:39,660
she went to parochial school.

324
00:18:39,760 --> 00:18:42,020
And the nuns made everybody stand up and asked them

325
00:18:42,120 --> 00:18:44,020
what their father did.

326
00:18:44,021 --> 00:18:45,560
And she got up, she didn't know much.

327
00:18:45,561 --> 00:18:46,820
She was 5 or 6 years old.

328
00:18:46,821 --> 00:18:49,620
She says, my dad's a sand hog.

329
00:18:49,621 --> 00:18:52,520
So nobody really knew what it was.

330
00:18:52,521 --> 00:18:55,500
And then the kids say, what does your dad do?

331
00:18:55,501 --> 00:18:57,100
He's a sand pig.

332
00:18:57,101 --> 00:18:59,660
And they got all mixed up.

333
00:18:59,661 --> 00:19:01,380
You tell most people what you do, most people,

334
00:19:01,381 --> 00:19:03,140
if you tell them you're a sand hog, they're what?

335
00:19:03,141 --> 00:19:05,700
Now people know because it's been more publicized,

336
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:07,640
these tunnels or whatever.

337
00:19:07,641 --> 00:19:12,380
Not too many people really know what a sand hog was.

338
00:19:12,381 --> 00:19:14,780
The first major construction project

339
00:19:14,781 --> 00:19:17,020
to use sand hog labor in New York

340
00:19:17,021 --> 00:19:19,680
was the laying of the foundations for the Brooklyn

341
00:19:19,681 --> 00:19:23,020
Bridge in the 1870s.

342
00:19:23,021 --> 00:19:25,800
The bridge was the brainchild of John Roebling,

343
00:19:25,801 --> 00:19:28,520
perhaps the most brilliant and daring engineer

344
00:19:28,521 --> 00:19:32,100
of his generation.

345
00:19:32,101 --> 00:19:34,660
He based his plan on pioneering work

346
00:19:34,661 --> 00:19:38,340
by Captain James Eads, who used compressed air

347
00:19:38,341 --> 00:19:40,180
to lay the foundations for a bridge

348
00:19:40,181 --> 00:19:43,580
the Mississippi River in the 1860s.

349
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:48,520
Queissons, or huge inverted wooden boxes weighing 16 tons

350
00:19:48,620 --> 00:19:51,400
each, were used to create the foundations for the bridge

351
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:53,920
towers.

352
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:56,220
The queissons were floated into position,

353
00:19:56,320 --> 00:20:00,400
sunk to the riverbed by placing huge stones on top of them,

354
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:02,700
and then filled with compressed air, which

355
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:06,380
displaced the water and mud.

356
00:20:06,480 --> 00:20:08,380
The sand hogs entered the queissons

357
00:20:08,381 --> 00:20:11,120
through an airlock, which maintained the air pressure

358
00:20:11,220 --> 00:20:13,620
within the chamber.

359
00:20:13,720 --> 00:20:16,220
As the sand hogs removed the muck at the bottom,

360
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:18,060
the caisson was gradually lowered

361
00:20:18,160 --> 00:20:21,320
until it finally rested on bedrock.

362
00:20:21,420 --> 00:20:24,120
The sand hogs frequently encountered large boulders

363
00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:27,360
that had to be broken up by hammer and pick.

364
00:20:27,460 --> 00:20:29,840
Eventually, they began using explosives,

365
00:20:29,940 --> 00:20:32,340
which added to the potential for danger.

366
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:35,840
But perhaps the principal hazard was a sudden drop

367
00:20:35,841 --> 00:20:38,180
in air pressure called a blowout.

368
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:44,020
Many construction workers were killed

369
00:20:44,120 --> 00:20:47,760
by what we call blowouts, where the pressures from the water

370
00:20:47,860 --> 00:20:51,160
and the soft material was such that whatever mining techniques

371
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:54,820
were used to restrain the opening they created by digging

372
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:57,300
were no longer strong enough, and they would collapse,

373
00:20:57,400 --> 00:21:00,040
and the river and the mud would enter the excavation,

374
00:21:00,140 --> 00:21:03,200
and many men drowned in the process.

375
00:21:03,300 --> 00:21:05,340
Another hazard was the debilitating

376
00:21:05,341 --> 00:21:09,580
an often lethal condition known variously as caisson's disease

377
00:21:09,680 --> 00:21:12,880
or the bends, which occurs when the human body

378
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:17,460
is subjected to rapid changes in air pressure.

379
00:21:17,560 --> 00:21:21,260
This 1930s film shows a sand hog entering a decompression

380
00:21:21,360 --> 00:21:23,520
chamber in order to prevent the bends.

381
00:21:26,860 --> 00:21:29,460
Without slow decompression, nitrogen bubbles

382
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:32,640
form in the bloodstream, causing severe cramps,

383
00:21:32,641 --> 00:21:36,740
fainting spells, or in extreme cases, death.

384
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:39,880
Relatively little was understood about this phenomenon

385
00:21:39,980 --> 00:21:43,280
in the 1870s, and few, if any, precautions were taken

386
00:21:43,380 --> 00:21:45,820
to protect the workers.

387
00:21:45,920 --> 00:21:48,320
And the workers, most of whom were recent immigrants,

388
00:21:48,420 --> 00:21:52,860
suffered in silence, afraid of losing their princely wages

389
00:21:52,960 --> 00:21:54,900
of $2 a day.

390
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,600
Many of them quite literally worked until they dropped dead,

391
00:21:58,700 --> 00:22:01,360
only to be replaced by another eager worker

392
00:22:01,361 --> 00:22:06,160
from the vast pool of immigrant labor.

393
00:22:06,260 --> 00:22:08,800
But the construction of the foundations of the Brooklyn

394
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:10,800
Bridge was an important model.

395
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:15,000
It set the stage for subsequent tunnel construction in New York.

396
00:22:15,100 --> 00:22:17,980
An engineer named DeWitt Clinton Haskins

397
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:20,780
reasoned that if a caisson of compressed air

398
00:22:20,880 --> 00:22:23,820
could move vertically, as it did in a foundation job,

399
00:22:23,920 --> 00:22:27,620
it could also move horizontally, as in a tunnel.

400
00:22:27,621 --> 00:22:31,660
And so in 1874, combining the compressed air technique

401
00:22:31,760 --> 00:22:35,720
with the tunneling shield, work began on a railroad tunnel

402
00:22:35,820 --> 00:22:40,960
beneath the Hudson River linking New York and New Jersey.

403
00:22:41,060 --> 00:22:43,860
Haskins Tunnel claimed many lives.

404
00:22:43,960 --> 00:22:49,480
Workers succumbed to the bends and died in numerous accidents.

405
00:22:49,580 --> 00:22:53,080
The most famous accident occurred in July of 1880,

406
00:22:53,180 --> 00:22:55,920
when a blowout claimed 20 lives.

407
00:22:55,921 --> 00:22:59,820
But the ranks of the sand hogs continued to grow.

408
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:02,720
Workers continued to suffer from the bends.

409
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:06,460
Blowouts and cave-ins and fires were a constant hazard.

410
00:23:10,960 --> 00:23:13,680
But as New York entered the 20th century,

411
00:23:13,780 --> 00:23:17,480
the demand for tunnels skyrocketed.

412
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:24,480
What makes New York unique is that it is basically an island.

413
00:23:24,481 --> 00:23:29,480
And the surrounding areas, whether they're Queens

414
00:23:29,580 --> 00:23:33,720
and Brooklyn and New Jersey, there's a lot of people

415
00:23:33,820 --> 00:23:36,020
that commute into Manhattan.

416
00:23:36,120 --> 00:23:38,220
It's the financial capital of the world.

417
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:46,000
Underground New York became a veritable maze of tunnels.

418
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:48,480
Railroad tunnels bored under the rivers,

419
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:52,080
cut and cover jobs created subways and sewers.

420
00:23:52,081 --> 00:23:55,520
And hard rock tunnels mined hundreds of feet beneath the

421
00:23:55,620 --> 00:23:59,380
surface to service the city's water supply.

422
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:02,120
And then there were the tunnels that had an enormous effect

423
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:05,120
on Manhattan by opening the island to thousands

424
00:24:05,220 --> 00:24:08,940
of commuters daily, the Holland and Lincoln Tunnels.

425
00:24:15,440 --> 00:24:19,280
By the 1920s, the automobile had become a permanent fixture

426
00:24:19,281 --> 00:24:23,680
to New York City's landscape.

427
00:24:23,780 --> 00:24:26,660
And as trucks took on an increasingly important role

428
00:24:26,760 --> 00:24:28,860
in the transportation of freight,

429
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:31,520
it became apparent that a vehicle link connecting

430
00:24:31,620 --> 00:24:34,900
Manhattan Island to the mainland was needed.

431
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:40,400
But there was a compelling reason for not building a bridge.

432
00:24:40,500 --> 00:24:42,500
Having just lived through World War I,

433
00:24:42,600 --> 00:24:46,000
military experts feared the potential for sabotage.

434
00:24:46,001 --> 00:24:53,000
The consideration is that if you had a terrorist explosion

435
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:57,420
or you got bombed and the bridge fell down,

436
00:24:57,520 --> 00:24:59,540
you'd block up the harbor.

437
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:03,380
So you don't do that with a tunnel.

438
00:25:03,480 --> 00:25:06,260
A tunnel seemed the obvious solution.

439
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:09,060
However, it would need to be larger and longer

440
00:25:09,160 --> 00:25:12,620
than any vehicular tunnel constructed to date.

441
00:25:12,621 --> 00:25:16,720
But the most complex problem for the chief engineer, Clifford

442
00:25:16,820 --> 00:25:20,600
Holland, a soft-spoken, precise Harvard graduate,

443
00:25:20,700 --> 00:25:24,000
was the lethal carbon monoxide in automobile exhaust.

444
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:31,980
The Holland Tunnel was really the first successful application

445
00:25:32,080 --> 00:25:34,980
of technology to deal with the issue of ventilation.

446
00:25:35,080 --> 00:25:37,320
Holland devised a ventilation system

447
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:41,020
powered by 84 enormous fans housed in buildings

448
00:25:41,021 --> 00:25:42,820
at either end of the tunnel.

449
00:25:42,920 --> 00:25:44,920
The fans blew fresh air through ducts

450
00:25:45,020 --> 00:25:46,960
beneath the tunnel's roadway.

451
00:25:47,060 --> 00:25:49,020
The air was then circulated through the tunnel

452
00:25:49,120 --> 00:25:52,760
and sucked out through other ducts in the roof.

453
00:25:52,860 --> 00:25:56,640
These fans are capable of removing all of the air

454
00:25:56,740 --> 00:26:00,200
within the tunnel within 60 seconds.

455
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:04,040
And that process really was uniquely developed

456
00:26:04,140 --> 00:26:06,780
for the Holland Tunnel and still remains

457
00:26:06,781 --> 00:26:13,080
the primary method of ventilating vehicular tunnels.

458
00:26:13,180 --> 00:26:16,460
Construction began on Columbus Day in 1920,

459
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:19,120
with crews working on both sides of the river,

460
00:26:19,220 --> 00:26:22,100
pushing simultaneously toward the middle.

461
00:26:22,200 --> 00:26:24,000
A total of four shields were used

462
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:27,160
to dig the twin 29-foot wide tubes,

463
00:26:27,260 --> 00:26:31,340
extending 8,463 feet from shore to shore.

464
00:26:31,341 --> 00:26:39,020
The two crews met up with each other on October 29, 1924.

465
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:42,020
But Chief Engineer Holland never got to see

466
00:26:42,120 --> 00:26:44,620
his crowning achievement completed.

467
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,080
He drove himself at such a feverish pace

468
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:51,220
that he fell ill and died of heart disease at age 41, just

469
00:26:51,320 --> 00:26:54,460
two days before the first of the two tubes was connected.

470
00:26:54,461 --> 00:26:55,360
was connected.

471
00:27:00,600 --> 00:27:03,840
In November 1927, the population of New Jersey

472
00:27:03,940 --> 00:27:05,600
had reached nearly 4 million.

473
00:27:05,700 --> 00:27:08,480
And the brand new Holland Tunnel would help thousands

474
00:27:08,580 --> 00:27:10,940
get to their jobs every day in New York City

475
00:27:11,040 --> 00:27:15,780
through twin tubes 93 feet under the Hudson River.

476
00:27:15,880 --> 00:27:18,420
It was the year Charles Lindbergh became the first person

477
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:20,120
to fly the Atlantic Ocean.

478
00:27:20,220 --> 00:27:23,920
The great Babe Ruth had just hit 60 home runs in one season.

479
00:27:23,921 --> 00:27:28,120
And Henry Ford was producing his 15 millionth car.

480
00:27:28,220 --> 00:27:31,760
The tunnel was designed to accommodate 1,900 vehicles

481
00:27:31,860 --> 00:27:34,160
per hour in each direction.

482
00:27:34,260 --> 00:27:38,060
Peak capacity of 22.3 million vehicles annually

483
00:27:38,160 --> 00:27:41,480
was expected by 1943.

484
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:44,480
On the day the tunnel opened, the line of pedestrians,

485
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:47,380
20,000 strong, marched through.

486
00:27:47,480 --> 00:27:50,320
The governors of the two states, New York's Al Smith

487
00:27:50,420 --> 00:27:52,680
and A. Harry Moore from New Jersey,

488
00:27:52,681 --> 00:27:56,820
stopped in the midway point to shake hands and pose for photos.

489
00:28:00,920 --> 00:28:04,020
Later that day, when the parade was over, cars were allowed in.

490
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:10,340
The first collision was reported one hour and 15 minutes later.

491
00:28:15,640 --> 00:28:19,380
The opening of the George Washington Bridge in 1931

492
00:28:19,381 --> 00:28:22,480
provided an additional thoroughfare for trans-Hudson

493
00:28:22,580 --> 00:28:23,820
traffic.

494
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:28,280
The passage of time had lessened concerns about sabotage.

495
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,360
And locating the bridge further upriver

496
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:32,060
near the northern tip of the island

497
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:34,260
meant that construction wouldn't interfere

498
00:28:34,360 --> 00:28:37,460
with harbor traffic.

499
00:28:37,560 --> 00:28:39,740
During the first five years of operation,

500
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:41,900
more than 8 million vehicles passed

501
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,380
through its massive steel arches.

502
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:46,140
But traffic patterns across the river

503
00:28:46,240 --> 00:28:48,000
continued to escalate.

504
00:28:48,001 --> 00:28:51,040
Greater access bred greater demand.

505
00:28:51,140 --> 00:28:53,900
And it wasn't long before the New York, New Jersey Port

506
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:57,840
Authority began planning a new tunnel.

507
00:28:57,940 --> 00:29:00,180
The Lincoln Tunnel was intended to supplement

508
00:29:00,280 --> 00:29:04,180
the two existing routes, so it was situated in the Midtown

509
00:29:04,280 --> 00:29:06,980
area.

510
00:29:07,080 --> 00:29:09,880
These rare films produced by the Port Authority

511
00:29:09,980 --> 00:29:12,800
during construction illustrate just how ambitious

512
00:29:12,900 --> 00:29:14,020
an undertaking it was.

513
00:29:14,021 --> 00:29:15,020
was.

514
00:29:15,021 --> 00:29:22,460
Let's join a crew of sand hogs as they start on the day's job under the river.

515
00:29:22,461 --> 00:29:26,980
They must stay in the airlock a few minutes until the air pressure is built up to equal

516
00:29:26,981 --> 00:29:29,200
that in which they will work.

517
00:29:29,201 --> 00:29:31,620
It is different when they return from work.

518
00:29:31,621 --> 00:29:36,600
Then they must be gradually decompressed, which takes a much longer time.

519
00:29:36,601 --> 00:29:42,140
This whole process is for the protection of compressed air workers.

520
00:29:42,141 --> 00:29:51,020
Inside the tunnel, under compressed air, while overhead, all sorts of river craft and great

521
00:29:51,021 --> 00:29:58,780
ocean liners sail by.

522
00:29:58,781 --> 00:30:10,460
Ready for a shove.

523
00:30:10,461 --> 00:30:20,120
push against the iron tunnel ring and move the shield forward 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 inches,

524
00:30:20,121 --> 00:30:23,575
making room for a new ring of tunnel segment.

525
00:30:23,576 --> 00:30:27,260
As the jacks push against the tunnel, the shield

526
00:30:27,261 --> 00:30:33,960
pushes forward, earth comes in through various openings, about 50% of the earth will come in,

527
00:30:33,961 --> 00:30:39,800
the other 50% will be displaced around the tunnel, and then that material is removed by means of

528
00:30:39,801 --> 00:30:44,414
trains that take this material out and is disposed of.

529
00:30:44,415 --> 00:30:48,600
The next operation would be to take prefabricated

530
00:30:48,601 --> 00:30:53,320
cast iron ring sections. Tunnel segments are stored at the site of construction.

531
00:30:54,040 --> 00:30:57,800
Before being sent down into the tunnel, they are inspected and cleaned,

532
00:30:58,360 --> 00:31:07,800
then loaded on cars and lowered by elevator.

533
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:23,800
Each segment weighs more than a ton and a half.

534
00:31:25,760 --> 00:31:33,240
These sections are brought in, attached to what is known as an erection arm.

535
00:31:33,241 --> 00:31:42,180
The erection arm then will provide proper positioning of the ring section.

536
00:31:42,181 --> 00:31:50,520
And when it's placed in position, that ring section is bolted, as you see, to allow the

537
00:31:50,521 --> 00:31:57,080
construction of another complete ring of the tunnel.

538
00:31:57,081 --> 00:32:04,080
Each tunnel ring is made up of 14 segments and the key piece.

539
00:32:08,480 --> 00:32:14,680
As many as 18 cycles, which means 18 shoves and 18 rings have been completed in a day's

540
00:32:14,681 --> 00:32:21,680
work driving through sills.

541
00:32:21,681 --> 00:32:25,520
It takes about an hour and a half to construct one ring.

542
00:32:30,940 --> 00:32:34,160
The key segment makes the ring complete.

543
00:32:37,400 --> 00:32:41,460
When that is completed, we really repeat the cycle again,

544
00:32:41,461 --> 00:32:44,920
and the jacks are placed against the new ring.

545
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:47,040
The jacks apply pressure.

546
00:32:47,640 --> 00:32:49,120
The shield is pushed forward,

547
00:32:49,700 --> 00:32:51,400
and as the shield is pushed forward,

548
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:56,160
we saw it progressing the construction of the tunnel to complete one tube of the tunnel

549
00:32:56,720 --> 00:33:02,400
two thousand three hundred and seventy rings are erected more than a quarter of a million

550
00:33:02,401 --> 00:33:08,400
boats hold the segments and rings together the surveyor's job is to keep the shield on the right

551
00:33:08,401 --> 00:33:15,840
course as it is pushed through the riverbed sandhogs work day and night building the iron tunnel shell

552
00:33:15,841 --> 00:33:20,020
At this point, one year's work has been accomplished.

553
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:25,220
Holding through, the two shields meet within a quarter of an inch.

554
00:33:25,320 --> 00:33:27,520
These men have something to celebrate.

555
00:33:29,860 --> 00:33:32,860
Today, the technology of shield-driven tunnels

556
00:33:32,960 --> 00:33:36,220
has been largely replaced by immersed tube tunnels.

557
00:33:38,860 --> 00:33:41,860
Eliminating the danger of cave-ins and the health hazards

558
00:33:41,960 --> 00:33:45,040
of working in compressed air, immersed tube tunnels

559
00:33:45,041 --> 00:33:48,480
are prefabricated on land.

560
00:33:48,580 --> 00:33:52,780
You're building the tunnel as a ship on launching waves.

561
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:59,180
And you then float it, tow it to the site where you outfit it.

562
00:33:59,280 --> 00:34:03,620
And then in a prepared trench, you sink it.

563
00:34:03,720 --> 00:34:05,560
And they're usually 300 foot long.

564
00:34:05,660 --> 00:34:07,700
And you put one section after the other,

565
00:34:07,800 --> 00:34:09,900
and you make a tunnel.

566
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:13,400
In this way, structurally sound underwater tunnels

567
00:34:13,401 --> 00:34:15,160
can be built efficiently.

568
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:17,880
And just as there have been advances in soft ground

569
00:34:17,980 --> 00:34:21,380
tunneling, so have there been advances in man's ability

570
00:34:21,480 --> 00:34:23,380
to bore through solid rock.

571
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:30,220
When we return, one of the most colossal tunneling jobs

572
00:34:30,320 --> 00:34:35,080
ever attempted, 800 feet below the streets of New York.

573
00:34:35,081 --> 00:34:40,080
New York.

574
00:34:40,081 --> 00:34:44,080
New York City sits on a labyrinth of tunnels.

575
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:50,080
Among all the tunnels that comprise the underground infrastructure of New York,

576
00:34:50,081 --> 00:34:57,080
some of the most spectacular are carved deep in the bedrock that underlies the skyscrapers and congested streets.

577
00:34:57,081 --> 00:35:02,080
These are New York City's water tunnels, and without them,

578
00:35:02,081 --> 00:35:05,120
Without them, the city simply could not sustain the people

579
00:35:05,220 --> 00:35:06,320
who live and work there.

580
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:13,820
The early days of the water system

581
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:19,300
go back to when Aaron Burr was forming a company to bring water

582
00:35:19,400 --> 00:35:21,900
to Manhattan in the late 1700s.

583
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:23,760
And he got the charter for a bank.

584
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:26,340
And it turned out that basically what he did was charter a bank,

585
00:35:26,440 --> 00:35:28,100
which became the Chase Manhattan Bank.

586
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:29,800
But he didn't do much water supply

587
00:35:29,801 --> 00:35:33,300
got a little angry, and they forced him to put in water

588
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:36,600
mains, and he ran miles of hollowed out wooden logs

589
00:35:36,700 --> 00:35:38,980
through lower Manhattan.

590
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:42,220
These wooden pipes were barely adequate.

591
00:35:42,320 --> 00:35:45,040
New Yorkers were left in precarious circumstances,

592
00:35:45,140 --> 00:35:50,020
still in great need of large supplies of fresh water.

593
00:35:50,120 --> 00:35:53,560
Fires regularly destroyed the wooden buildings in the densely

594
00:35:53,660 --> 00:35:54,960
crowded neighborhoods.

595
00:35:54,961 --> 00:35:57,860
neighborhoods.

596
00:35:57,960 --> 00:36:01,660
One fire in 1835, which began in a department store

597
00:36:01,760 --> 00:36:04,700
in the Wall Street area, burned for more than 24 hours

598
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:07,400
and destroyed over 600 buildings.

599
00:36:07,500 --> 00:36:11,980
The estimated cost of the damage was $25 million.

600
00:36:12,080 --> 00:36:15,580
And so in 1835, New York finally approved construction

601
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:17,620
of a reservoir and aqueduct system

602
00:36:17,720 --> 00:36:22,320
fed by the Croton River in Westchester County.

603
00:36:22,321 --> 00:36:25,760
An aqueduct delivered the water to a midtown reservoir

604
00:36:25,860 --> 00:36:29,620
situated at the modern-day site of the New York Public Library,

605
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:31,660
42nd Street and Fifth Avenue.

606
00:36:34,560 --> 00:36:37,060
This increased supply of clean water

607
00:36:37,160 --> 00:36:40,280
allowed the population to grow and the city to expand.

608
00:36:40,380 --> 00:36:43,400
And by 1907, with New York City's population

609
00:36:43,500 --> 00:36:46,580
approaching 4 million, it became apparent

610
00:36:46,680 --> 00:36:49,080
that demand was outrunning supply.

611
00:36:52,320 --> 00:36:54,920
Work began on a new reservoir system designed

612
00:36:55,020 --> 00:36:58,460
to tap water resources in the Catskill mountain region,

613
00:36:58,560 --> 00:37:00,260
80 miles to the north.

614
00:37:00,360 --> 00:37:03,560
And in 1907, construction started on a tunnel

615
00:37:03,660 --> 00:37:07,460
to bring the water to the city.

616
00:37:07,560 --> 00:37:09,800
New York City and the engineers and planners

617
00:37:09,900 --> 00:37:13,140
felt that if they came from the high elevations in the mountains

618
00:37:13,240 --> 00:37:14,440
and brought water through a tunnel,

619
00:37:14,540 --> 00:37:16,840
it would be that the old water seeks its own level.

620
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:19,540
They would be able to build a tunnel, build some shafts,

621
00:37:19,640 --> 00:37:22,080
drop the water down, carry the water underground,

622
00:37:22,081 --> 00:37:25,020
and bring it back to New York and build a shaft at the other end.

623
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:26,720
And the water, seeking its own level,

624
00:37:26,820 --> 00:37:29,120
would come up through the tunnel without any pumping.

625
00:37:29,220 --> 00:37:32,260
And so basically, 90% of the water in the city of New York,

626
00:37:32,360 --> 00:37:36,260
in fact, more than 90%, requires no energy costs to the city

627
00:37:36,360 --> 00:37:38,840
to deliver.

628
00:37:38,940 --> 00:37:42,260
City Water Tunnel Number One, which went into service

629
00:37:42,360 --> 00:37:46,140
in 1917, runs from the Hillview Reservoir in Westchester

630
00:37:46,240 --> 00:37:49,980
County south through the Bronx, crossing under the Harlem River

631
00:37:49,981 --> 00:37:52,980
down the length of Manhattan, back under the East River

632
00:37:53,080 --> 00:37:56,280
to its termination in the Fort Greene section of Brooklyn.

633
00:37:58,980 --> 00:38:01,720
A second tunnel, completed in 1937,

634
00:38:01,820 --> 00:38:05,120
serves the boroughs of Queens and Brooklyn.

635
00:38:05,220 --> 00:38:09,540
The rock underlying New York is an extremely hard composite

636
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:11,840
of quartz, mica, and feldspar.

637
00:38:11,940 --> 00:38:14,340
Tunneling through it was an immense undertaking,

638
00:38:14,440 --> 00:38:16,740
requiring enormous amounts of dynamite

639
00:38:16,840 --> 00:38:19,980
to blast through the stubborn rock.

640
00:38:19,981 --> 00:38:24,720
Many sand hogs lost their lives working on the water tunnels.

641
00:38:24,820 --> 00:38:28,420
In fact, 60 men died during one three-year span,

642
00:38:28,520 --> 00:38:31,720
three lives lost for every mile driven.

643
00:38:34,920 --> 00:38:39,240
But their sacrifice made New York's water system possible.

644
00:38:39,340 --> 00:38:42,040
However, there are indications that the two tunnels

645
00:38:42,140 --> 00:38:45,180
are now leaking badly.

646
00:38:45,280 --> 00:38:47,180
Some would say hemorrhaging.

647
00:38:49,980 --> 00:38:53,680
City tunnel number one was put in service in 1917

648
00:38:53,780 --> 00:38:56,320
and has never been taken out of service.

649
00:38:56,420 --> 00:39:00,320
City tunnel number two was put in service in 1937,

650
00:39:00,420 --> 00:39:02,160
and that's never been taken out of service.

651
00:39:02,260 --> 00:39:03,700
And now we're modernizing the system.

652
00:39:03,800 --> 00:39:06,160
We're building a third delivery tunnel so we can go back

653
00:39:06,260 --> 00:39:09,200
and refurbish the other two.

654
00:39:09,300 --> 00:39:12,240
City engineers admit they have no idea

655
00:39:12,340 --> 00:39:16,100
whether the tunnels are obstructed or in danger of collapse.

656
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:17,640
If either of the water tunnels were

657
00:39:17,641 --> 00:39:19,940
were to suffer a serious disruption,

658
00:39:20,040 --> 00:39:23,140
it would be a disaster of unparalleled scope,

659
00:39:23,240 --> 00:39:26,320
possibly requiring the evacuation of at least half

660
00:39:26,420 --> 00:39:31,220
of New York's population for up to a year.

661
00:39:31,320 --> 00:39:34,360
People go and talk that most of the population of the city

662
00:39:34,460 --> 00:39:37,760
doesn't even know what's going on, where the water comes from,

663
00:39:37,860 --> 00:39:40,860
and how important it is to have this here tunnel finish

664
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:43,200
before the other one gives way.

665
00:39:43,300 --> 00:39:45,000
Because if that then it'd be a disaster,

666
00:39:45,100 --> 00:39:46,640
the city becomes a ghost town.

667
00:39:46,641 --> 00:39:47,540
No water.

668
00:39:47,541 --> 00:39:50,140
You got your fire department, your hospitals,

669
00:39:50,141 --> 00:39:52,880
your drinking water, your bathing.

670
00:39:52,881 --> 00:39:54,940
It's gone.

671
00:39:54,941 --> 00:39:57,620
Faced with this dangerous possibility,

672
00:39:57,621 --> 00:40:01,120
New York City determined to build a third water tunnel

673
00:40:01,121 --> 00:40:05,420
at a projected cost of over $5 billion.

674
00:40:05,421 --> 00:40:09,300
Water tunnel number three easily eclipses the price tag

675
00:40:09,301 --> 00:40:12,760
of the $1.2 billion World Trade Center,

676
00:40:12,761 --> 00:40:16,300
the $320 million Verrazano Narrows Bridge,

677
00:40:16,301 --> 00:40:19,440
and the $90 million Brooklyn Battery Tunnel.

678
00:40:19,540 --> 00:40:23,440
It is, in fact, the largest public works project New

679
00:40:23,540 --> 00:40:28,040
York City has ever seen.

680
00:40:28,140 --> 00:40:31,940
The completed tunnel will be 55 miles long.

681
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:37,260
At its widest, it will have a diameter of 24 feet.

682
00:40:37,360 --> 00:40:41,660
At its deepest, it is 800 feet below the street.

683
00:40:41,760 --> 00:40:44,700
If one were to turn the Chrysler Building upside down,

684
00:40:44,701 --> 00:40:50,340
its Art Deco spire would just pierce the tunnel ceiling.

685
00:40:50,440 --> 00:40:53,280
It is scheduled for completion in the year 2025

686
00:40:53,380 --> 00:40:56,740
and will be capable of conveying approximately 1 billion

687
00:40:56,840 --> 00:41:00,120
gallons of water daily.

688
00:41:00,220 --> 00:41:04,380
The construction of this tunnel is a monumental undertaking.

689
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:07,860
But one tool that is helping to maximize the efficiency

690
00:41:07,960 --> 00:41:12,260
of the project is the TBM, or tunnel boring machine,

691
00:41:12,261 --> 00:41:14,060
sometimes referred to as the mole.

692
00:41:16,960 --> 00:41:21,200
Moles are powerful, mechanized rock-crushing engines

693
00:41:21,300 --> 00:41:24,900
capable of pulverizing huge quantities of rock

694
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:28,780
that previously had to be blasted with dynamite.

695
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:32,180
While boring, the cutters are forced into the rock face.

696
00:41:32,280 --> 00:41:34,780
Their rolling action and the propeller's force

697
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:39,760
fractures the rock as the cutter head rotates.

698
00:41:39,761 --> 00:41:42,360
We have 40 cutters on the cutter head,

699
00:41:42,460 --> 00:41:44,240
individual roller cutters.

700
00:41:44,340 --> 00:41:48,840
And the machine is capable of about 4 million pounds of thrust.

701
00:41:48,940 --> 00:41:53,540
We're using maybe around 3.2, 3.3 million pounds of thrust

702
00:41:53,640 --> 00:41:54,480
right now.

703
00:41:54,580 --> 00:41:57,520
And that presses into the rock, causes a fracture,

704
00:41:57,620 --> 00:42:00,580
and two adjacent cutters will make a chip form

705
00:42:00,680 --> 00:42:03,320
or a piece fall out.

706
00:42:03,420 --> 00:42:07,220
Moles are capable of actually walking themselves

707
00:42:07,221 --> 00:42:10,120
on a set of retractable hydraulic feet.

708
00:42:10,220 --> 00:42:14,360
The operator also uses these feet to wedge the mole firmly

709
00:42:14,460 --> 00:42:17,160
in place during the cutting cycle.

710
00:42:17,260 --> 00:42:19,260
Moles can cut through rock faster

711
00:42:19,360 --> 00:42:22,960
and require fewer workers than conventional mining techniques.

712
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:28,980
Well, right now, there's about 25 people employed underground,

713
00:42:29,080 --> 00:42:31,080
the contractor's forces.

714
00:42:31,180 --> 00:42:34,520
There may have been about 1 1⁄2 times that many

715
00:42:34,620 --> 00:42:37,220
if we had a drill and blast operation.

716
00:42:37,221 --> 00:42:39,920
The technology may have changed somewhat

717
00:42:40,020 --> 00:42:42,720
from the early days of hard rock tunneling,

718
00:42:42,820 --> 00:42:47,000
but it still requires a strong back and nerves of steel,

719
00:42:47,100 --> 00:42:50,800
and something else that the sand hogs have in abundance,

720
00:42:50,900 --> 00:42:55,780
a deep sense of pride in the work they do.

721
00:42:55,880 --> 00:42:59,300
This is going to be like a Roman castle as far as I go.

722
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:02,180
It's going to stand for at least 200 or 300 years

723
00:43:02,280 --> 00:43:04,280
we'll be using this water supply.

724
00:43:04,380 --> 00:43:05,940
It sounds kind of corny, but you also

725
00:43:05,941 --> 00:43:07,700
but you also give something that's

726
00:43:07,800 --> 00:43:10,220
going to make this city a better place for a long time.

727
00:43:10,320 --> 00:43:14,220
And the people in the 1800s and 1840s that started working

728
00:43:14,320 --> 00:43:18,080
on the water supply, I'm sure they felt some of that.

729
00:43:18,180 --> 00:43:20,560
And the people today hope that hundreds of years from now,

730
00:43:20,660 --> 00:43:22,960
people will think that they may have been associated

731
00:43:23,060 --> 00:43:26,560
with the building of this infrastructure

732
00:43:26,660 --> 00:43:28,660
for the city of New York.

733
00:43:28,760 --> 00:43:31,860
Here, at the Van Cortlandt Park valve chamber,

734
00:43:31,960 --> 00:43:35,340
500 feet below the streets of the Bronx,

735
00:43:35,341 --> 00:43:39,340
there is tangible evidence that the goal of a safe, reliable

736
00:43:39,440 --> 00:43:43,880
water supply for the city of New York is now within reach.

737
00:43:43,980 --> 00:43:46,980
This immense chamber could easily accommodate

738
00:43:47,080 --> 00:43:51,480
St. Patrick's Cathedral within its massive expanse.

739
00:43:51,580 --> 00:43:55,200
And to those who built it, it is a cathedral of sorts,

740
00:43:55,300 --> 00:44:01,000
a subtle but powerful monument to sand hogs past and present,

741
00:44:01,001 --> 00:44:06,640
and to an awesome endeavor that began nearly 5,000 years ago.

742
00:44:06,740 --> 00:44:08,940
In fact, all of the world's great tunnels

743
00:44:09,040 --> 00:44:12,940
resonate with the collective genius of the people who,

744
00:44:13,040 --> 00:44:17,080
through sweat and sacrifice, have given the world

745
00:44:17,180 --> 00:44:18,920
this modern marvel.

